we're gonna turn to employees at  meatpacking plants who are telling us  that they are worried about their health  but they cannot afford to lose their  jobs an ongoing investigation found  there are at least 14,000 coronavirus  cases tied to 181 plants in the United  States and at least 54 workers have died  our consumer investigative correspondent  and a Warner spoke to employees who say  they are in desperate need of their  paychecks including bonus money what  some are calling hazard pay and a good  morning good morning  advocates say many workers are afraid to  speak out fearing they may jeopardize  those paychecks those who did told us  that even so some improvements have been  made they still are afraid and they  don't feel safe inside the plants Oh  baby huh Maria didn't want us to show  her face or give her last name but her  stress shows through  she works in Grand Island Nebraska at  the jbs meatpacking plant where many  workers have fallen ill from coronavirus  on April 28th one of them died her close  friend 61 year old Reinaldo Ramirez  Betty said him he said good partner good  man he dies Ryan Brown works there too  what is it like for you in your job in  terms of a line how close are you to  other people we are very we're pretty  snug there are some places where you are  literally like shoulder to shoulder with  somebody some 3,600 people work at the  jbs plant which processes 1 billion  pounds of beef each year the company  says it's doing everything it can to  provide a safe working environment still  emails we obtained from the Regional  Health Department show at the end of  March a local emergency room doctor  called the plant a hotspot another wrote  jbs should shut down for two weeks that  never happened  by April 21st the Health Department  reported more than 230 jbs related cases  on its website jbs cites measures taken  to protect workers including temperature  checks and face masks along with  increased sanitation and physical  distances  but Brown says sometimes there's no real  feasible way to keep us all six feet  apart it's just a lot of people in one  space you know what I mean it's just  very congested to limit that congestion  jbs and producers like Tyson Foods are  taking other steps this Tyson video  shows improvements including workstation  dividers on-site medical screenings and  social distancing Meatpacking companies  have also increased pay for workers  during the crisis  Tyson announced two five hundred dollar  bonuses in May and July jbs has raised  hourly pay and is giving a six hundred  dollar bonus to workers who had no more  than two unexcused absences between  April 20th and May 15th but Mark Peron  president of the UFCW International  Union which represents many workers is  skeptical they want people to come to  work so they're paying them more money  the problem inside the plant jbs workers  who stay home because they fear  contracting the virus won't lose their  jobs the company says but they also will  not be paid which Peron says leaves many  workers with no option but to work  because they need their paychecks  doesn't necessarily mean you're  expendable and I think that people has  started to think about these workers  especially in these packing houses is  being expendable but George Washington  University expert Melissa Perry says  keeping workers safe protects the  meatpacking business as well I think  it's the economy  to frame it as though it's a choice  between workers or our meat that's  really not the issue the issue is it's a  choice between insuring city protections  within meat processing plants so that we  can continue to have a steady reliable  supply of meat back at jbs Ryan Brown is  making four dollars more per hour now  but says money isn't the answer where  you're putting people's families like a  risk and like their lives at risk I  don't think that there's you can put a  number on that on its website jbs says  it is encouraging sick team members to  stay home from  work and it is relaxed its attendance  policies they also pointed out that no  one has asked them to close the plant  local health officials say that's  because they couldn't get support from  state officials to do that we reached  out to the governor's office but never  heard back Dale thank you and I'm struck  by Ryan Browns words nobody wants to be  snug during this pandemic and you cannot  put a price on your life thank you very  much
 

